WEBVTT

00:00.700 --> 00:04.370
So we were doing part c of the
first problem on the Calculus

00:04.370 --> 00:09.210
BC exam, the free response
part, and so what, I'll re-read

00:09.210 --> 00:12.420
it, it says the region
r, this is the region r,

00:12.420 --> 00:13.610
is the base of a solid.

00:13.610 --> 00:16.190
I redrew the region r here, but
with a little perspective, so

00:16.190 --> 00:19.930
we can hopefully visualize
it in three dimensions.

00:19.930 --> 00:22.590
So it says for this solid, each
cross section perpendicular

00:22.590 --> 00:24.000
to the x-axis, right?

00:24.000 --> 00:25.750
There's two ways you can do a
cross-section, you could do

00:25.750 --> 00:27.870
them, you could cut this
way, but that would be

00:27.870 --> 00:30.040
parallel to the x-axis.

00:30.040 --> 00:33.210
We wanted to cut perpendicular
to the x-axis, or parallel

00:33.210 --> 00:34.420
to the y-axis, right?

00:34.420 --> 00:38.740
So if we cut it along
the line like that.

00:38.740 --> 00:42.500
So they say, each cross-section
perpendicular to the

00:42.500 --> 00:43.810
x-axis is a square.

00:43.810 --> 00:45.120
So I drew a couple
of squares here.

00:45.120 --> 00:46.250
That's one.

00:46.250 --> 00:50.270
So here, this would be the
base, and since we know the

00:50.270 --> 00:52.290
cross-section is the square,
the height has to be the

00:52.290 --> 00:53.710
same length as the base.

00:53.710 --> 00:54.660
Same thing here.

00:54.660 --> 00:56.790
So here, the height will be
really high, because this is

00:56.790 --> 01:01.150
kind of maybe our maximum point
in terms of the base width.

01:01.150 --> 01:04.090
But it's still pretty wide,
then it gets narrow again.

01:04.090 --> 01:06.320
So how do we figure out
the volume of this solid?

01:06.320 --> 01:08.140
Which is kind of
hard to visualize.

01:08.140 --> 01:10.816
And it's in some ways you
easier to visualize it than

01:10.816 --> 01:12.680
it is to draw, so you have
to give me some credit.

01:12.680 --> 01:15.830
But anyway, what we do is, we
take the area of each of these

01:15.830 --> 01:17.960
squares, and I drew
one of them here.

01:17.960 --> 01:20.520
You take you each of the area
of each of these squares,

01:20.520 --> 01:24.600
multiply them by a super small
change in x, and that we know,

01:24.600 --> 01:27.770
from everything we've learned
in calculus hopefully, that

01:27.770 --> 01:30.180
that super small change in x,
I'm trying to draw a little

01:30.180 --> 01:32.830
perspective, is dx.

01:32.830 --> 01:36.960
So if we multiply dx times the
area of this square, that is

01:36.960 --> 01:42.190
the volume of this kind of
part the entire solid.

01:42.190 --> 01:45.900
And if we were to sum up all of
these infinitely thin solids,

01:45.900 --> 01:47.830
we would get the volume
for the whole thing.

01:47.830 --> 01:49.540
So how do we do that?

01:49.540 --> 01:53.850
Well let's write our
integral expression.

01:53.850 --> 01:59.350
So what is the area of
each of these squares?

01:59.350 --> 02:02.020
Each of these cross-sections,
what is the area?

02:02.020 --> 02:05.090
Well the base is going to
be the difference between

02:05.090 --> 02:06.520
our two functions, right?

02:06.520 --> 02:13.210
This top function right
here, that was sin of pi x.

02:13.210 --> 02:18.560
And this bottom function, right
here, that is y is equal to

02:18.560 --> 02:22.010
x to the third minus 4x.

02:22.010 --> 02:27.040
So the base of the functions,
the base of this distance right

02:27.040 --> 02:29.560
here is going to be the
difference between the top

02:29.560 --> 02:31.820
function and the
bottom function.

02:31.820 --> 02:42.540
So each base is going to be sin
of pi x, minus this function,

02:42.540 --> 02:46.580
so minus x to the third, plus
4x, right switch the sign.

02:46.580 --> 02:50.590
Minus x to the third, plus 4x.

02:50.590 --> 02:54.110
So far this might look pretty
similar to part a, but

02:54.110 --> 02:55.280
what's the twist here?

02:55.280 --> 02:56.980
We want the area.

02:56.980 --> 02:58.350
The area of each of
the squares, not

02:58.350 --> 02:59.200
just this distance.

02:59.200 --> 02:59.940
So what's the area?

02:59.940 --> 03:03.260
It's going to be this distance
squared, so we have to

03:03.260 --> 03:06.360
square this entire thing.

03:06.360 --> 03:09.090
So that's the area of each
of these squares, and then

03:09.090 --> 03:12.740
we have to multiply them
times a little bit of dx.

03:12.740 --> 03:16.950
And that gives us the volume
for each of these, I guess,

03:16.950 --> 03:18.690
parts of the entire solid.

03:18.690 --> 03:20.360
And then what are our
boundaries of integration?

03:20.360 --> 03:22.340
Well it's the same
thing as in part a.

03:22.340 --> 03:24.240
This is 0, this is 2.

03:24.240 --> 03:28.090
So the boundaries are
pretty straightforward.

03:28.090 --> 03:31.970
So we essentially just have
to evaluate this now.

03:31.970 --> 03:36.730
And just like in part b, I've
first tried to evaluate this

03:36.730 --> 03:39.620
analytically, and you end up
getting a very, very,

03:39.620 --> 03:41.000
very hairy integral.

03:41.000 --> 03:43.220
Which you can do analytically,
you but you have to know some

03:43.220 --> 03:46.010
power reduction formulas in
trigonometry, you have to do

03:46.010 --> 03:49.160
some integration by parts, it
would probably use up all of

03:49.160 --> 03:51.220
the time on the AP Exam.

03:51.220 --> 03:54.080
